SPACE COP

“In space, jurisdiction is a suggestion.”

The movie opens with complete silence.

Earth hangs in the void.

Beautiful. Serene.

Then a police siren starts.

Not through space.

Just somehow in space.

A 1998 Crown Victoria rockets past the camera with blue lights flashing, rear bumper held on with duct tape.

Nicolas Cage is driving.

No helmet.

Window down.

Aviators on.

He is pursuing a stolen lunar rover across the Sea of Tranquility.

Dispatch crackles:

“Unit 7, be advised, suspect is approaching the international boundary.”

Cage grabs the radio.

“THERE ARE NO INTERNATIONAL BOUNDARIES ON THE MOON.”

Dispatch:

“Exactly. Disengage.”

Cage leans forward.

“That means he can’t escape jurisdiction.”

“Officer Cage, that is literally the opposite—”

Cage throws the radio out the window.

It floats alongside the car.

He looks at it.

“…I’m gonna need that.”

He reaches out.

Misses.

Opening title:

SPACE COP

Electric guitar.

⸻

Nicolas Cage plays Detective Rex Orion Cage, the first—and somehow only—police officer assigned to the moon.

Nobody knows which government employs him.

Including payroll.

His badge says:

LUNAR MUNICIPAL POLICE
BADGE 001

Jessica, now inexplicably his civilian forensic consultant, looks at it.

“Municipality of what?”

Cage:

“The moon.”

“The entire moon?”

He looks offended.

“Crime doesn’t respect zoning.”

Little Nick is his partner.

He has a tiny spacesuit.

The helmet is basically a Christmas ornament.

His badge says:

001½

He fucking hates it.

“I’m senior tactical.”

Cage:

“You’re eight inches tall.”

“I CLEARED THE AIR VENT HOSTAGE CRISIS.”

Jessica:

“You got stuck in a dryer.”

“It was a hostile rotating environment.”

Micro Nick lives in the glove box.

Technically Internal Affairs.

Nobody appointed him.

He just periodically opens the glove box and says things like:

“Questionable.”

Then closes it.

⸻

Their precinct is an abandoned lunar research station converted into the Moon Police Department.

It contains:

one desk,

two chairs,

a fax machine,

and a jail cell that absolutely should not work in one-sixth gravity.

Every morning Cage walks into briefing.

There is nobody else there.

He stands in front of a corkboard.

“Alright, listen up.”

Jessica:

“Nicolas, it’s just us.”

Cage:

“Then attendance is excellent.”

He flips over the board.

Crime statistics.

Illegal oxygen sales: up 14%.

Lunar trespassing: rampant.

Unauthorized flag relocation: 3 incidents.

Moon rocks stolen: technically all of them.

Jessica:

“Who are we policing?”

Cage points at Earth.

“Everybody.”

⸻

The first major case begins when someone steals the moon’s shadow.

Jessica:

“That isn’t possible.”

Cage:

“Then explain this.”

He opens the blinds.

Sunlight everywhere.

Even on the dark side.

Jessica stares.

“…Okay.”

Little Nick:

“Somebody boosted nighttime.”

Cage slowly puts on his aviators.

“Black market.”

Jessica:

“For darkness?”

Cage:

“You’d be surprised what people pay for privacy.”

Suddenly Micro Nick crawls out of the glove box.

“Vice.”

Everyone looks at him.

He holds up a microscopic Polaroid.

On it:

a nightclub built inside a crater.

Sign outside:

THE ECLIPSE

Little Nick:

“Shit.”

Cage:

“You know it?”

Little Nick:

“I know the bartender.”

Jessica:

“You know a bartender on the moon?”

Little Nick:

“I had a life before you people.”

⸻

They arrive at The Eclipse.

The bouncer is an enormous Russian cosmonaut named Boris wearing mirrored goggles.

He looks down at Cage.

“Members only.”

Cage flashes badge.

“Law enforcement.”

Boris:

“Not member.”

Cage flashes Costco card.

Boris immediately opens velvet rope.

Jessica:

“…Seriously?”

Boris:

“Executive membership.”

Cage nods.

“Respect recognizes respect.”

Inside, lunar criminals are doing zero-gravity shots while a DJ spins records vertically because gravity can’t stop him.

Cage spots their suspect.

Dr. Lance Nebula.

Former astrophysicist.

Current space pimp.

Cape.

Single leather glove.

Owns a moon limousine.

Jessica:

“That is the most obviously guilty man I’ve ever seen.”

Cage:

“That’s what worries me.”

Little Nick:

“Too much garnish.”

They approach.

Nebula smiles.

“Detective Cage.”

Cage:

“Doctor Nebula.”

Nebula:

“You’re outside your jurisdiction.”

Cage looks around.

At the moon.

Then back at him.

“I’m standing on it.”

Nebula:

“This club is registered to Malta.”

Jessica:

“HOW?”

Nebula:

“Flag.”

He points.

Tiny Maltese flag taped to bar.

Cage:

“…Son of a bitch.”

Little Nick:

“He knows maritime law.”

Jessica:

“We’re on the fucking moon.”

Little Nick:

“Then he knows space maritime law.”

“That isn’t a thing.”

From behind them, an attorney:

“It is now.”

